## {{ entry.version }}{% if entry.date %} ({{ entry.date }}){% endif %}

{% if entry.version == "v0.1.0" %}
### Release status

v0.1.0 is cut before the comparator gates (maintainer decision 2026-11-10). It has **not** cleared the three gates of org golden rule 6 (a fidelity sheet from its source, the per-PR delivery smoke, and an anchor reproducing the source's own trend). The anchor is pending, and so is the ns-3 contrib module `hopnet`: it is still being written, and an `ns3/` directory in this release's archive, if there is one, is that work in progress as it stood on `main`. No result from this release counts as a benchmark number, and it does not enter the benchmarks suite until a release clears the gates; that gated release is proposed as v0.2.0 (swarm-routing/.github ROADMAP "Releases"). It ships `core/` (MIT OR GPL-2.0-only, with `core/LICENSE`) and `docs/` (plus `ns3/` if it exists), as they stand on `main` at the release commit.

{% elif entry.version == "v0.2.0" %}
### Release status

v0.2.0 is the "gated" release of `docs/roadmap.md` (maintainer decision 2026-11-24, #198): each gate of org golden rule 6 has given its verdict, and **the anchor's verdict is negative**. The thesis's headline trend (Wang 2007, Fig. 5.11: HOPNET delivering more than AntHocNet as the network scales) is **not reproduced** by this reconstruction. Three pre-registered pilot rounds against AntHocNet missed their threshold, so the anchor is recorded as a written infeasibility verdict (`docs/anchor-verdict.md`, #5, #126) and the anchor campaign itself was not run. **HOPNET's numbers from this release do not count in comparisons** and do not enter the benchmarks suite. The other gates passed: the fidelity sheet (`docs/fidelity.md`) and the per-PR delivery smoke (`tools/bench/campaigns/smoke.yaml`, first run `smoke-47db292`, #28). It ships `core/` (MIT OR GPL-2.0-only, with `core/LICENSE`), the ns-3 contrib module `hopnet` in `ns3/` (GPL-2.0-only) with the swarm-core router observer (#40), the benchmark pipeline and `docs/`. Every reading stays selectable through its `core::Config` switch; reopening the anchor needs a source that settles what the verdict lists as unrecoverable (#21, the 2009 journal paper).

